{
 "_": "Per-package test budgets for the CI timing gate (kata b6rv, see docs/developing-evener/testing.md, \"The Test Timing Ratchet\"). \"packages\" holds each Go package's test CPU seconds: the user+system time of its test binary (and every child it waited for), which go test -exec evener-dev test-cpu-exec reports, or for a sharded package (agent, cmd/evener-hub, cmd/evener) its shard processes' CPU summed. \"wallPackages\" holds each row's wall seconds: the package-level Elapsed of go test -json, a sharded package's shard phase, and the frontend's vitest assertion durations under the single key \"web\". scripts/gate/test-timing-budget.sh fails a package at 1.5x its CPU budget and warns at 1.1x, fails it at 2.5x its wall budget (the backstop for a package that got slower by waiting), and holds a row with no CPU measurement (web) to 1.5x/1.1x of its wall budget; failure limits scale by the run's median ratio, CPU for CPU limits and wall for wall limits and class ceilings. The top-level \"metric\" marker is what keeps the ratios enforced; without it every ratio downgrades to a warning. Each number is the slowest seen across recent green CI runs of the tests lanes on main, floored at 2s for Go packages. Raise a budget by editing this file in the same commit as the change that earned the increase, or run `make test-rebaseline` on a CI-class host. Emptying both maps puts the ratchet back in its no-baseline state, where the gate stays warn-only everywhere, CI included.",
 "perTestCeilingSeconds": 3,
 "_perTestCeilingClasses": "Per-test ceilings segmented by test class (#4601, Jesse's ruling 2026-10-13). A test whose name is listed in a class - or a subtest of one (Name/sub) - is compared STRICTLY against that class's ceiling, and a breach FAILs the check under --check in a CI-shaped environment or --strict. A test named in no class keeps the flat perTestCeilingSeconds above and stays warn-only in every mode, --strict included, so the unclassified backlog cannot red the gate. ceilingSeconds = the union of the class's largest CI observations across run 38458180180 and run 38468494246 (jobs tests / root, tests / nonroot, web-unit) + 25%, rounded up to a multiple of five seconds (#4601). The first run set the original ceilings; the first strict run (the second) breached install, transcript large-fixture and retirement, and brought live-stack E2E and dev-tool compile/CLI within 12% of theirs, so those five are the two runs' union max + 25%. A 2026-10-14 ruling (issue #4615) removed four runner-bound classes whose wall time measures the runner, not the code - dev-tool compile/CLI, install, transcript large-fixture and gate-runner integration - so their members now keep the flat perTestCeilingSeconds above and stay warn-only in every mode, like the rest of the unclassified backlog; the sighting was #4615's TestCrossCompileWindows at 52.26s against its 15s class ceiling on a 67-line diff in internal/transcriptindex that could not cost 37 seconds. A 2026-10-16 follow-up (issue #4699) moved TestLoadSessionJobActivityTree_TuesdayFullyFoldsAndFullyPaginates out of session-activity and onto the same warn-only default: it folds 200k events and pages through every one of them, and it runs t.Parallel() in the agent package, so its wall time tracks that package's parallel load rather than its own code - measured alone it takes 7.6-9.6s on a workstation at load 17, while CI read 30.09-32.28s for the same code, and the agent package gained 174 test declarations in the days after this class was blessed. The class keeps its other two members, which stay strict. Two further classes are deliberately absent: bounded-wait unit gets no ceiling of its own yet (a wall ceiling there mostly measures the runner, so its members stay unclassified and warn-only on the flat 3s), and the web repo-audit test was a class of one, split into one case per slice of the tracked TypeScript test files (#4608) rather than blessed. A 2026-10-27 follow-up moved TestE2E_RetireOverlappedByAnotherOwnershipAction off live-stack E2E onto the same warn-only default: it started one hub-and-daemon stack per subtest, so its wall time was the sum of three runner-bound stack starts (6.8s typical, 15.3-16.9s on a loaded runner across six runs) and sat on either side of the 15s ceiling; its three cases now run concurrently, so its wall time is the slowest case (about 1-2s) and it stays unclassified. A test may be listed in only ONE class; a name matched by two classes is a config error the check FAILs on.",
